e full design being used.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nKey assumptions for factorial and fractional factorial designs include:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variance (constant variance across levels of factors).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between factors and the response.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nThese designs are particularly useful when researchers wish to explore the effects of multiple factors on a response variable efficiently. They are applicable in various fields, including agriculture, manufacturing, and pharmaceuticals, where understanding interactions between factors is crucial.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis typically involves conducting an Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of main effects and interactions. Post-hoc tests may be employed to further investigate significant factors.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Factorial designs allow for the examination of interactions between factors, providing a comprehensive understanding of the system.\n- Fractional factorial designs reduce the number of experimental runs required, saving time and resources while still yielding significant insights.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Full factorial designs can become impractical with a large number of factors due to the exponential increase in the number of runs required.\n- Fractional factorial designs may lead to aliasing of effects, making it difficult to distinguish between main effects and interactions.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methodologies include response surface methodology (RSM) for optimization and Taguchi methods for robust design. Alternatives to factorial designs include completely randomized designs and block designs, which may be more suitable in certain contexts.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing these designs, researchers should ca